// New items for verbal, written to the official CSC scope. Append new items at the END only.
export const ITEMS = [
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word TERSE most nearly mean?\n\"When reporters asked about the flood budget, the barangay captain gave a terse reply: \"No comment yet.\"\"","o":["brief and abrupt","angry and loud","polite and detailed","confused and unclear"],"a":0,"e":"\"No comment yet\" is a very short, clipped answer, so terse means brief and abrupt.","x":{"steps":["Look at the clue right after the colon: the reply was only three words.","Ask what kind of reply that is: short and to the point.","Pick the choice that describes a short, curt answer."],"wrong":{"angry and loud":"Nothing in the sentence shows shouting or anger; the reply is simply short.","polite and detailed":"A three-word reply is the opposite of detailed.","confused and unclear":"\"No comment yet\" is clear; it is just short."},"tip":"Use the example given in the sentence; it usually shows the meaning of the word."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word TABLE most nearly mean?\n\"The committee agreed to table the proposal until the budget office submits its report.\"","o":["approve immediately","postpone for later","print and distribute","reject completely"],"a":1,"e":"\"Until the budget office submits its report\" shows the proposal is set aside for now, so table means postpone.","x":{"steps":["Notice the word \"until\": something is waiting for a later event.","A proposal that waits for a report is being put off, not decided.","Choose the meaning that fits \"wait until later.\""],"wrong":{"approve immediately":"Approving immediately would not need to wait for the report.","print and distribute":"Table here is a verb about a decision, not about printing papers.","reject completely":"Rejecting is a final decision; \"until\" shows the matter will come back."},"tip":"Common words can have special meanings; let the rest of the sentence decide."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word FRUGAL most nearly mean?\n\"Mrs. Dumaguing was known for her frugal habits; she reused envelopes and repaired her own umbrella rather than buy a new one.\"","o":["careless with money","generous to others","sparing in spending","fond of collecting"],"a":2,"e":"Reusing envelopes and repairing instead of buying new shows she avoids spending, so frugal means sparing or thrifty.","x":{"steps":["Read the examples after the semicolon.","Both examples show saving money instead of buying.","Pick the meaning that matches careful, minimal spending."],"wrong":{"careless with money":"Careless spenders buy new things; she repairs old ones.","generous to others":"The examples are about saving, not giving to others.","fond of collecting":"Reusing envelopes is about saving, not collecting things."},"tip":"A semicolon often introduces examples that explain the word before it."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word CORROBORATED most nearly mean?\n\"The witness's account was corroborated by the CCTV footage, so the investigator no longer doubted her.\"","o":["contradicted","questioned","delayed","confirmed"],"a":3,"e":"The investigator stopped doubting her because the footage supported her story; corroborated means confirmed.","x":{"steps":["Find the result clause: \"so the investigator no longer doubted her.\"","Ask what the footage must have done to remove the doubt.","It must have supported her account, so the word means confirmed."],"wrong":{"contradicted":"If the footage contradicted her, the investigator would doubt her more.","questioned":"Questioning her story would not remove the investigator's doubt.","delayed":"Delaying the account has nothing to do with removing doubt."},"tip":"Cause-and-effect words like \"so\" let you work backward to the meaning."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word RETICENT most nearly mean?\n\"The new clerk was reticent during her first staff meeting, offering an opinion only when the chief asked her directly.\"","o":["reluctant to speak","eager to please","unprepared for work","quick to disagree"],"a":0,"e":"She spoke only when asked directly, which describes someone who holds back from talking; reticent means reluctant to speak.","x":{"steps":["Look at the phrase that describes her behavior: \"offering an opinion only when ... asked directly.\"","Someone who speaks only when asked is quiet and reserved.","Choose the meaning that matches holding back from speaking."],"wrong":{"eager to please":"Nothing shows she was trying to please anyone; she simply kept quiet.","unprepared for work":"The sentence is about speaking up, not about being prepared.","quick to disagree":"She gave few opinions, so she was not quick to argue."},"tip":"Reticent = keeps thoughts to oneself; do not confuse it with \"reluctant\" in general."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word PALLIATIVE most nearly mean?\n\"The municipal engineer warned that patching the cracks was only a palliative; the real problem lay in the bridge's foundation.\"","o":["a permanent solution","a short-term relief","a costly mistake","a careful inspection"],"a":1,"e":"\"Only\" and \"the real problem lay\" show that patching eases the symptom without fixing the cause; a palliative is short-term relief.","x":{"steps":["Notice the word \"only\": patching is less than a real fix.","The second clause says the real problem is somewhere else.","So patching just eases things for now; choose short-term relief."],"wrong":{"a permanent solution":"The engineer says the real problem remains, so it is not permanent.","a costly mistake":"Nothing says patching wasted money; it simply does not solve the cause.","a careful inspection":"Patching is a repair, not an inspection."},"tip":"\"Only a ___ ; the real problem ...\" signals something that treats symptoms, not causes."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word RESPITE most nearly mean?\n\"After three weeks of heavy rain, the farmers welcomed the respite that the sunny weekend brought.\"","o":["harvest","short break or relief","strong wind","extra work"],"a":1,"e":"After weeks of rain, a sunny weekend gives a pause from the bad weather; respite means a short break or relief.","x":{"steps":["Contrast the situation: weeks of rain, then a sunny weekend.","The farmers \"welcomed\" it, so it is something good.","A pleasant pause from hardship is a short break or relief."],"wrong":{"harvest":"A sunny weekend does not by itself bring a harvest.","strong wind":"Strong wind is not something sunny weather brings, and farmers would not welcome it.","extra work":"Farmers would not \"welcome\" extra work after hard weeks."},"tip":"Words like \"welcomed\" tell you whether the unknown word is positive or negative."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Word meaning","q":"In the sentence below, what does the word AMBIGUOUS most nearly mean?\n\"Critics called the proposal ambiguous because it could be read as either raising or freezing the fees.\"","o":["expensive to carry out","written in haste","open to more than one meaning","unfair to the poor"],"a":2,"e":"The proposal \"could be read as either\" of two things, so it has more than one possible meaning.","x":{"steps":["Find the reason after \"because.\"","\"Could be read as either A or B\" means two readings are possible.","Choose the meaning about having more than one interpretation."],"wrong":{"expensive to carry out":"Cost is never mentioned in the sentence.","written in haste":"Haste might cause unclear writing, but the clue is about two possible meanings.","unfair to the poor":"Fairness is not mentioned; the issue is unclear meaning."},"tip":"The word \"because\" introduces the definition clue; read it closely."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Despite the long queue at the city hall, the applicants remained ______ and waited for their numbers to be called.\"","o":["patient","furious","absent","hurried"],"a":0,"e":"\"Despite the long queue\" and \"waited\" show the applicants stayed calm; patient fits.","x":{"steps":["\"Despite\" signals a contrast with the long queue.","They \"waited for their numbers,\" so they stayed calm.","Choose the word for calm waiting: patient."],"wrong":{"furious":"Furious people would not calmly wait; the contrast word \"despite\" points the other way.","absent":"They were in the queue, so they were not absent.","hurried":"Hurried does not match people who simply waited for their turn."},"tip":"\"Despite\" means the answer goes against what the first part would make you expect."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The nurse reminded the mothers that the vaccines are given ______ of charge at the health center.\"","o":["freely","free","freed","freedom"],"a":1,"e":"The fixed expression is \"free of charge,\" meaning without payment.","x":{"steps":["Recognize the common phrase \"___ of charge.\"","The correct idiom is \"free of charge.\"","Other word forms change the expression and make it wrong."],"wrong":{"freely":"\"Freely of charge\" is not an English expression.","freed":"\"Freed\" is a verb form meaning released; it does not fit.","freedom":"\"Freedom of charge\" is not an expression; freedom is a noun about liberty."},"tip":"Memorize set phrases: free of charge, in charge of, at no cost."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The report was so ______ that the board members finished reading it in five minutes.\"","o":["lengthy","concise","technical","confidential"],"a":1,"e":"Finishing in five minutes means the report was short and to the point; concise fits the so...that result.","x":{"steps":["Look at the result after \"that\": they finished in five minutes.","What quality of a report lets you read it that fast? Being short.","Choose concise (short and clear)."],"wrong":{"lengthy":"A lengthy report would take longer than five minutes.","technical":"Technical reports are usually slower to read, not faster.","confidential":"Being confidential does not affect how fast it is read."},"tip":"In \"so ___ that,\" the result tells you exactly what the blank must be."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Unlike his older brother, who spends every peso he earns, Ramon is quite ______.\"","o":["wasteful","wealthy","thrifty","generous"],"a":2,"e":"\"Unlike\" sets up a contrast with a brother who spends everything, so Ramon saves money: thrifty.","x":{"steps":["\"Unlike\" means Ramon is the opposite of his brother.","The brother spends every peso; the opposite is someone who saves.","Choose thrifty."],"wrong":{"wasteful":"Wasteful describes the brother, not the contrast.","wealthy":"Being rich is not the opposite of spending everything.","generous":"Generous means giving to others; the contrast is about saving versus spending."},"tip":"\"Unlike,\" \"but,\" and \"whereas\" call for an opposite idea."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The school postponed the outdoor program ______ the typhoon signal raised over the province.\"","o":["in spite of","instead of","because of","regardless of"],"a":2,"e":"The typhoon signal is the reason for postponing, so the cause connector \"because of\" fits.","x":{"steps":["Ask how the two ideas relate: postponing and a typhoon signal.","The typhoon signal caused the postponement.","Choose the connector that shows cause: because of."],"wrong":{"in spite of":"\"In spite of\" shows contrast; schools postpone because of storms, not despite them.","instead of":"\"Instead of\" shows replacement, which makes no sense here.","regardless of":"\"Regardless of\" means the signal did not matter, but it clearly did."},"tip":"Decide first whether the link is cause, contrast or replacement, then pick the connector."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The two barangays had quarreled over their boundary for years, but the new agreement finally brought about a ______ between them.\"","o":["rivalry","reconciliation","separation","competition"],"a":1,"e":"\"But\" and \"finally\" show the quarrel ended; a reconciliation is the restoring of friendly relations.","x":{"steps":["The first clause describes years of quarreling.","\"But ... finally\" signals a change for the better.","The opposite of a long quarrel is making peace: reconciliation."],"wrong":{"rivalry":"A rivalry continues the conflict instead of ending it.","separation":"They already had a boundary dispute; an agreement brings them together, not apart.","competition":"Competition is another form of conflict, not its resolution."},"tip":"\"But finally\" usually points to a resolution of the problem mentioned first."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The engineer's explanation was so ______ with technical terms that the residents left the meeting more confused than before.\"","o":["sparse","laden","plain","brief"],"a":1,"e":"The residents were confused, so the explanation was heavily loaded with jargon; \"laden with\" means full of.","x":{"steps":["The result is confusion, so the explanation had too much jargon.","The blank must mean \"full of\" and work with the word \"with.\"","\"Laden with\" means heavily loaded with, so it fits."],"wrong":{"sparse":"Sparse means few; few technical terms would not confuse the residents.","plain":"A plain explanation would make things clearer, not more confusing.","brief":"Brief describes length, and \"brief with technical terms\" is not idiomatic."},"tip":"Check that the word fits both the meaning and the preposition after the blank."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Far from being ______, the new policy was adopted only after two years of consultations with every sector.\"","o":["careful","popular","costly","hasty"],"a":3,"e":"\"Far from being\" introduces the opposite of the truth; two years of consultation means it was not hasty.","x":{"steps":["\"Far from being X\" means \"not X at all.\"","The policy took two years and consulted everyone, so it was slow and deliberate.","So it was far from hasty."],"wrong":{"careful":"Two years of consultation shows it WAS careful, so \"far from careful\" contradicts the sentence.","popular":"Popularity is not discussed; the clue is about time taken.","costly":"Cost is not mentioned anywhere in the sentence."},"tip":"\"Far from being ___\" needs the opposite of what the rest of the sentence proves."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"Applicants must submit the documents on or ______ March 15; late submissions will not be accepted.\"","o":["after","before","since","until"],"a":1,"e":"Late submissions are rejected, so the deadline is \"on or before March 15.\"","x":{"steps":["Read the second clause: late submissions are not accepted.","So documents must arrive no later than March 15.","The standard phrase for a deadline is \"on or before.\""],"wrong":{"after":"\"On or after\" would allow late submissions, which the notice forbids.","since":"\"Since\" refers to a starting point in the past, not a deadline.","until":"\"On or until\" is not the standard way to state a deadline."},"tip":"Deadlines in notices use \"on or before\"; start dates use \"on or after.\""}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence completion","q":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.\n\"The mayor's speech was meant to ______ the fears of vendors who thought the market would be closed for good.\"","o":["arouse","confirm","ignore","allay"],"a":3,"e":"A mayor would want to calm the vendors' fears, and \"allay\" means to lessen or put to rest.","x":{"steps":["Identify the purpose: the speech was \"meant to\" do something about fears.","A leader speaking to worried vendors would try to calm them.","Allay means to calm or reduce fears."],"wrong":{"arouse":"Arouse means to stir up; the speech was not meant to create more fear.","confirm":"Confirming the fears would mean the market really closes, which is not the intent shown.","ignore":"You do not give a speech in order to ignore something."},"tip":"Allay fears, allay doubts: allay always means to calm or reduce."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"Each of the volunteers who joined the clean-up drive were given a certificate by the barangay.\"","o":["Each of the volunteers","who joined","the clean-up drive","were given a certificate by the barangay"],"a":3,"e":"The subject is \"Each,\" which is singular, so the verb should be \"was given,\" not \"were given.\"","x":{"steps":["Find the true subject: \"Each\" (not \"volunteers,\" which is inside the of-phrase).","\"Each\" is always singular.","A singular subject needs a singular verb: \"was given.\""],"wrong":{"Each of the volunteers":"\"Each of the volunteers\" is a correct subject phrase.","who joined":"\"Who joined\" correctly agrees with \"volunteers,\" its antecedent.","the clean-up drive":"\"The clean-up drive\" is a correct noun phrase."},"tip":"Each, every, either, neither, one: singular subjects, singular verbs."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"When the inspector arrived at the market yesterday, most of the stalls are already closed.\"","o":["When the inspector arrived","at the market yesterday","most of the stalls","are already closed"],"a":3,"e":"The sentence is about yesterday, so the verb must be past: \"were already closed.\"","x":{"steps":["Find the time marker: \"yesterday.\"","The first clause uses the past tense (\"arrived\").","The second verb must also be past: \"were,\" not \"are.\""],"wrong":{"When the inspector arrived":"\"Arrived\" is correct past tense for an event yesterday.","at the market yesterday":"\"At the market yesterday\" is a correct phrase of place and time.","most of the stalls":"\"Most of the stalls\" is a correct plural subject."},"tip":"Keep tenses consistent with the time words in the sentence."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"The supervisor asked Liza and I to prepare the minutes of the meeting.\"","o":["The supervisor asked","Liza and I","to prepare the minutes","of the meeting"],"a":1,"e":"\"Liza and I\" is the object of \"asked,\" so the object pronoun is needed: \"Liza and me.\"","x":{"steps":["Remove \"Liza and\" and test: \"The supervisor asked I\" sounds wrong.","\"Asked me\" is correct because the pronoun receives the action.","So it should be \"Liza and me.\""],"wrong":{"The supervisor asked":"\"The supervisor asked\" is a correct subject and verb.","to prepare the minutes":"\"To prepare the minutes\" is a correct infinitive phrase.","of the meeting":"\"Of the meeting\" is a correct prepositional phrase."},"tip":"Cover the other name; the pronoun that sounds right alone is right in the pair."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"All establishments must comply to the new fire safety rules before they renew their permits.\"","o":["All establishments must","comply to","the new fire safety rules","before they renew their permits"],"a":1,"e":"The correct idiom is \"comply with,\" not \"comply to.\"","x":{"steps":["Check each verb-preposition pair.","The verb \"comply\" always takes \"with.\"","So \"comply to\" is the error."],"wrong":{"All establishments must":"\"All establishments must\" is correct; \"establishments\" is plural and \"must\" fits any subject.","the new fire safety rules":"\"The new fire safety rules\" is a correct noun phrase.","before they renew their permits":"\"They\" correctly refers to the establishments and \"renew\" agrees with it."},"tip":"Comply with, agree with, consist of, insist on: learn verb-preposition pairs as units."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"If the contractor would have finished the road on time, the farmers could have brought their harvest to town.\"","o":["If the contractor would have finished","the road on time","the farmers could have brought","their harvest to town"],"a":0,"e":"In a past unreal conditional, the if-clause uses the past perfect: \"If the contractor had finished.\"","x":{"steps":["This sentence imagines a past that did not happen (third conditional).","Pattern: If + had + past participle, ... would/could have + past participle.","\"Would have finished\" in the if-clause is wrong; use \"had finished.\""],"wrong":{"the road on time":"\"The road on time\" is a correct object and time phrase.","the farmers could have brought":"\"Could have brought\" is the correct result-clause form.","their harvest to town":"\"Their harvest to town\" correctly refers to the farmers."},"tip":"Never put \"would have\" in the if-clause; use \"had\" + past participle."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"Along the shoreline of Barangay Dalisay Norte stands a row of mangroves and an old lighthouse.\"","o":["Along the shoreline","of Barangay Dalisay Norte","stands a row","of mangroves and an old lighthouse"],"a":2,"e":"The subject comes after the verb: \"a row of mangroves and an old lighthouse\" is a compound subject joined by \"and,\" so the verb should be \"stand.\"","x":{"steps":["In an inverted sentence, find the subject after the verb.","The subject is \"a row (of mangroves) and an old lighthouse\": two things joined by \"and.\"","Two things need a plural verb: \"stand,\" not \"stands.\""],"wrong":{"Along the shoreline":"\"Along the shoreline\" is a correct opening phrase of place.","of Barangay Dalisay Norte":"\"Of Barangay Dalisay Norte\" correctly names the place.","of mangroves and an old lighthouse":"The nouns themselves are correct; the problem is the verb that agrees with them."},"tip":"Flip an inverted sentence to normal order before checking agreement."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"The applicant whom the panel believed was the most qualified withdrew her application last week.\"","o":["The applicant whom","the panel believed","was the most qualified","withdrew her application"],"a":0,"e":"The pronoun is the subject of \"was the most qualified\" (\"the panel believed she was ...\"), so it must be \"who,\" not \"whom.\"","x":{"steps":["Treat \"the panel believed\" as an interrupter and set it aside.","What is left: \"___ was the most qualified.\"","The pronoun is the subject of \"was,\" so use \"who\" (like \"she,\" not \"her\")."],"wrong":{"the panel believed":"\"The panel believed\" is a correct inserted clause.","was the most qualified":"\"Was the most qualified\" correctly uses the superlative.","withdrew her application":"\"Withdrew her application\" is correct past tense with a matching pronoun."},"tip":"Who = he/she (subject); whom = him/her (object). Skip \"I think/they believed\" before deciding."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"By the time the ambulance reached the hospital, the doctors already prepared the operating room.\"","o":["By the time","the ambulance reached the hospital","the doctors","already prepared the operating room"],"a":3,"e":"The preparing happened before the ambulance arrived, so the earlier action needs the past perfect: \"had already prepared.\"","x":{"steps":["\"By the time\" signals that one past action finished before another.","The doctors prepared the room first; the ambulance arrived later.","The earlier past action takes \"had\": \"had already prepared.\""],"wrong":{"By the time":"\"By the time\" is a correct time expression.","the ambulance reached the hospital":"\"Reached\" is correct simple past for the later action.","the doctors":"\"The doctors\" is a correct subject."},"tip":"\"By the time + past\" calls for \"had + past participle\" in the other clause."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"Neither the cashier nor the clerks knows where the missing receipts were kept.\"","o":["Neither the cashier","nor the clerks","knows where","the missing receipts were kept"],"a":2,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, \"clerks,\" which is plural, so it should be \"know.\"","x":{"steps":["Find the two subjects joined by neither...nor: \"cashier\" and \"clerks.\"","The verb follows the subject closer to it: \"clerks.\"","\"Clerks\" is plural, so the verb is \"know,\" not \"knows.\""],"wrong":{"Neither the cashier":"\"Neither the cashier\" correctly opens the correlative pair.","nor the clerks":"\"Nor the clerks\" correctly pairs with \"neither.\"","the missing receipts were kept":"\"Were kept\" correctly agrees with the plural \"receipts.\""},"tip":"Neither/nor, either/or: the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"No one in the office types faster than him, so he was assigned to encode the forms.\"","o":["No one in the office","types faster than him","so he was assigned","to encode the forms"],"a":1,"e":"\"Than\" introduces a shortened clause: \"faster than he (types).\" The subject pronoun \"he\" is needed.","x":{"steps":["Complete the comparison in your head: \"faster than ___ types.\"","The pronoun is the subject of the understood verb \"types.\"","Subjects take \"he,\" so \"than him\" is the error."],"wrong":{"No one in the office":"\"No one in the office\" is a correct singular subject.","so he was assigned":"\"So he was assigned\" correctly uses \"he\" as a subject.","to encode the forms":"\"To encode the forms\" is a correct infinitive phrase."},"tip":"After \"than\" or \"as,\" finish the sentence in your head to choose the pronoun."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"The residents insisted for a meeting with the mayor because they were angry about the delay.\"","o":["The residents insisted for","a meeting with the mayor","because they were angry","about the delay"],"a":0,"e":"The verb \"insist\" takes \"on\": \"insisted on a meeting.\"","x":{"steps":["Check each verb-preposition pair.","\"Insist\" goes with \"on,\" not \"for.\"","So the first part contains the error."],"wrong":{"a meeting with the mayor":"\"A meeting with the mayor\" is a correct phrase.","because they were angry":"\"Because they were angry\" correctly uses a plural verb for \"they.\"","about the delay":"\"Angry about\" is a correct pairing for a situation."},"tip":"Insist ON, depend ON, rely ON: these verbs never take \"for.\""}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Error recognition","q":"Which part of this sentence contains an error?\n\"Not only did the river overflowed, but the bridge near the plaza also collapsed.\"","o":["Not only did","the river overflowed","but the bridge near the plaza","also collapsed"],"a":1,"e":"After the helping verb \"did,\" the main verb must be in base form: \"did the river overflow.\"","x":{"steps":["\"Not only\" at the start causes inversion with \"did.\"","\"Did\" already shows past tense.","So the main verb must be the base form: \"overflow,\" not \"overflowed.\""],"wrong":{"Not only did":"\"Not only did\" is the correct inverted opening.","but the bridge near the plaza":"\"But the bridge near the plaza\" correctly continues the not only...but also pair.","also collapsed":"\"Also collapsed\" is correct past tense in the second clause, which is not inverted."},"tip":"Do/does/did + base verb, always (did go, did overflow, never did overflowed)."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the power outage is correctly constructed?","o":["Because the generator broke down during the storm.","The generator broke down during the storm, the office closed early.","The generator broke down during the storm, so the office closed early.","The generator broke down during the storm the office closed early."],"a":2,"e":"Two complete thoughts are correctly joined by a comma plus the conjunction \"so.\"","x":{"steps":["Check whether each choice is a complete thought.","If it has two complete thoughts, check how they are joined.","A comma alone or nothing at all is wrong; a comma plus and/but/so is correct."],"wrong":{"Because the generator broke down during the storm.":"This is a fragment: \"Because...\" leaves the thought unfinished.","The generator broke down during the storm, the office closed early.":"This is a comma splice: two sentences joined by only a comma.","The generator broke down during the storm the office closed early.":"This is a run-on: two sentences with no punctuation or conjunction."},"tip":"Two complete sentences need a period, a semicolon, or a comma + FANBOYS word."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence describing the clerk training is correctly constructed?","o":["The training covers drafting memos, filing records, and how to answer calls.","The training covers drafting memos, filing records, and answering calls.","The training covers to draft memos, filing records, and answering calls.","The training covers drafting memos, to file records, and answer calls."],"a":1,"e":"All three items in the list use the same -ing form: drafting, filing, answering.","x":{"steps":["Find the list of items after \"covers.\"","Check that every item has the same grammatical form.","Only \"drafting, filing, answering\" keeps all items parallel."],"wrong":{"The training covers drafting memos, filing records, and how to answer calls.":"\"How to answer calls\" breaks the pattern of -ing words.","The training covers to draft memos, filing records, and answering calls.":"\"To draft\" does not match \"filing\" and \"answering,\" and \"covers to draft\" is ungrammatical.","The training covers drafting memos, to file records, and answer calls.":"This mixes three different forms: drafting, to file, answer."},"tip":"Items in a list must wear the same \"uniform\": all -ing, all to-verbs, or all nouns."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the tourists at the seawall is correctly constructed?","o":["Walking along the seawall, the tourists found the sunset beautiful.","Walking along the seawall, the sunset looked beautiful to the tourists.","The sunset looked beautiful walking along the seawall to the tourists.","Walking along the seawall, beautiful was the sunset for the tourists."],"a":0,"e":"The opening phrase \"Walking along the seawall\" must describe the word right after the comma; only \"the tourists\" can walk.","x":{"steps":["Ask: who is doing the walking?","The tourists walk, not the sunset.","The doer must come right after the introductory phrase."],"wrong":{"Walking along the seawall, the sunset looked beautiful to the tourists.":"This says the sunset was walking along the seawall (dangling modifier).","The sunset looked beautiful walking along the seawall to the tourists.":"The phrase \"walking along the seawall\" is misplaced and seems to describe the sunset.","Walking along the seawall, beautiful was the sunset for the tourists.":"After the comma comes \"beautiful,\" not the walkers, so the modifier still dangles."},"tip":"An opening -ing phrase must be followed immediately by the person doing the action."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the lost wallet is correctly constructed?","o":["The guard returned the wallet to the owner that he found in the canteen.","The guard returned to the owner that he found in the canteen the wallet.","The guard returned the wallet that he found in the canteen to its owner.","Found in the canteen, the guard returned the wallet to its owner."],"a":2,"e":"The clause \"that he found in the canteen\" sits right next to \"wallet,\" the thing that was found.","x":{"steps":["Ask what was found in the canteen: the wallet.","The describing clause must be placed next to the word it describes.","Choose the sentence where \"that he found...\" follows \"wallet.\""],"wrong":{"The guard returned the wallet to the owner that he found in the canteen.":"This suggests the guard found the owner in the canteen.","The guard returned to the owner that he found in the canteen the wallet.":"The word order is awkward and still attaches the clause to \"owner.\"","Found in the canteen, the guard returned the wallet to its owner.":"This says the guard was found in the canteen (dangling modifier)."},"tip":"Put describing words as close as possible to the word they describe."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the class suspension is correctly constructed?","o":["The rain was so heavy and classes were suspended in the whole province.","The rain was very heavy that classes were suspended in the whole province.","The rain was so heavy, therefore classes were suspended in the whole province.","The rain was so heavy that classes were suspended in the whole province."],"a":3,"e":"The correct pattern is \"so + adjective + that + result.\"","x":{"steps":["Notice that each choice shows a cause (heavy rain) and a result (suspension).","The pattern for this is \"so ... that.\"","Choose the sentence that has both \"so\" and \"that.\""],"wrong":{"The rain was so heavy and classes were suspended in the whole province.":"\"So heavy and\" leaves \"so\" without its partner \"that.\"","The rain was very heavy that classes were suspended in the whole province.":"\"Very\" cannot pair with \"that\"; it must be \"so ... that.\"","The rain was so heavy, therefore classes were suspended in the whole province.":"This is a comma splice, and \"so\" still lacks \"that.\""},"tip":"So + adjective + THAT + result: \"so tired that,\" \"so heavy that.\""}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the school inspection is correctly constructed?","o":["Neither the principal or the teachers were informed of the inspection.","Neither the principal nor the teachers was informed of the inspection.","Neither the principal nor the teachers has been informed of the inspection.","Neither the principal nor the teachers were informed of the inspection."],"a":3,"e":"\"Neither\" pairs with \"nor,\" and the verb agrees with the nearer subject \"teachers,\" so \"were\" is correct.","x":{"steps":["Check the pair: neither goes with nor.","Find the subject nearer the verb: \"teachers\" (plural).","Plural subject, plural verb: \"were informed.\""],"wrong":{"Neither the principal or the teachers were informed of the inspection.":"\"Neither\" must pair with \"nor,\" not \"or.\"","Neither the principal nor the teachers was informed of the inspection.":"\"Was\" is singular, but the nearer subject \"teachers\" is plural.","Neither the principal nor the teachers has been informed of the inspection.":"\"Has\" is singular, but the nearer subject \"teachers\" is plural."},"tip":"Neither...nor: the verb listens to the closer subject."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the payroll delay is correctly constructed?","o":["Submitting the reports late have caused delays in the payroll.","Submitting the reports late has caused delays in the payroll.","To submitting the reports late has caused delays in the payroll.","Submitted the reports late has caused delays in the payroll."],"a":1,"e":"A gerund phrase used as a subject (\"Submitting the reports late\") is singular, so it takes \"has.\"","x":{"steps":["Find the subject: the whole phrase \"Submitting the reports late.\"","A gerund phrase names one activity, so it is singular.","Singular subject: \"has caused,\" not \"have caused.\""],"wrong":{"Submitting the reports late have caused delays in the payroll.":"\"Reports\" is not the subject; the activity of submitting is, so \"have\" is wrong.","To submitting the reports late has caused delays in the payroll.":"\"To submitting\" mixes an infinitive marker with a gerund.","Submitted the reports late has caused delays in the payroll.":"\"Submitted\" is a past verb, not a noun, so the sentence has no proper subject."},"tip":"A gerund subject is one activity, so it always takes a singular verb."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the road repair is correctly punctuated and constructed?","o":["The road was repaired last month; however, it is already full of potholes.","The road was repaired last month, however, it is already full of potholes.","The road was repaired last month however it is already full of potholes.","The road was repaired last month. However it is already full, of potholes."],"a":0,"e":"\"However\" joining two complete sentences needs a semicolon before it and a comma after it.","x":{"steps":["See that both parts are complete sentences.","\"However\" is a transition word, not a conjunction like \"but.\"","Use a semicolon before \"however\" and a comma after it."],"wrong":{"The road was repaired last month, however, it is already full of potholes.":"A comma before \"however\" creates a comma splice.","The road was repaired last month however it is already full of potholes.":"With no punctuation, this is a run-on sentence.","The road was repaired last month. However it is already full, of potholes.":"\"However\" needs a comma after it, and the comma before \"of potholes\" wrongly splits the phrase."},"tip":"Sentence ; however, sentence. (Transition words cannot join sentences with just a comma.)"}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the farmers' program is correctly constructed?","o":["The program aims not only to train farmers but also helping them sell their produce.","The program not only aims to train farmers but also to help them sell their produce.","The program aims not only to train farmers but also to help them sell their produce.","The program aims to not only train farmers but also helping them sell their produce."],"a":2,"e":"What follows \"not only\" (to train) matches what follows \"but also\" (to help), so the pair is parallel.","x":{"steps":["Look at the word right after \"not only\" and right after \"but also.\"","They must be the same form.","Only \"not only to train ... but also to help\" matches."],"wrong":{"The program aims not only to train farmers but also helping them sell their produce.":"\"To train\" does not match \"helping.\"","The program not only aims to train farmers but also to help them sell their produce.":"\"Not only\" sits before the verb \"aims,\" but \"but also\" is followed by \"to help,\" so they are not parallel.","The program aims to not only train farmers but also helping them sell their produce.":"\"Train\" and \"helping\" are different forms."},"tip":"Not only X but also Y: X and Y must be the same kind of word or phrase."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Sentence structure","q":"Which sentence about the job applicants is a complete and correct sentence?","o":["The applicants who passed the written test and the interview.","Who passed the written test and the interview will be notified by email.","The applicants, who passed the written test, and the interview will be notified by email.","The applicants who passed the written test and the interview will be notified by email."],"a":3,"e":"It has a subject (applicants), a relative clause describing them, and a main verb (will be notified).","x":{"steps":["Find the subject and the main verb of each choice.","\"Who passed...\" describes the subject; it is not the main verb.","Only the last choice has the main verb \"will be notified\" with a clear subject."],"wrong":{"The applicants who passed the written test and the interview.":"This is a fragment: it has a subject but no main verb.","Who passed the written test and the interview will be notified by email.":"This has no clear subject; \"Who\" cannot start a statement this way.","The applicants, who passed the written test, and the interview will be notified by email.":"The commas make \"the interview\" part of the subject, so the interview is being notified."},"tip":"A complete sentence needs a subject and a main verb outside the \"who/which/that\" clause."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. Then, fill out the application form and attach a photocopy of your valid ID.\n2. First, get a queue number from the guard at the entrance of the municipal hall.\n3. Finally, wait for your name to be called at the releasing window.\n4. Once your form is checked, pay the fee at the cashier and keep the official receipt.","o":["1-2-4-3","2-1-4-3","2-4-1-3","2-1-3-4"],"a":1,"e":"The signal words give the order: First (2), Then (1), Once your form is checked (4), Finally (3).","x":{"steps":["Find the sentence with \"First\": sentence 2.","\"Then\" (1) comes next, since you fill out the form.","\"Once your form is checked\" (4) needs the form to exist, so it follows 1.","\"Finally\" (3) ends the paragraph."],"wrong":{"1-2-4-3":"Sentence 1 begins with \"Then,\" so it cannot come first.","2-4-1-3":"You cannot pay after your form is checked (4) before filling out the form (1).","2-1-3-4":"\"Finally\" (3) must be the last step, not the third."},"tip":"In process paragraphs, follow the signal words: first, then, next, once, finally."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. These seedlings were then planted along the riverbank by volunteers from three schools.\n2. Last June, the Barangay San Teodoro council launched a project to stop the erosion of the riverbank.\n3. Within a year, their roots had already begun holding the soil in place.\n4. The council first asked the municipal nursery to donate two thousand bamboo seedlings.","o":["2-1-4-3","4-2-1-3","2-4-3-1","2-4-1-3"],"a":3,"e":"Sentence 2 introduces the project; 4 mentions the seedlings; 1 says \"These seedlings\" were planted; 3 says \"their roots\" held the soil.","x":{"steps":["Sentence 2 introduces the project and the council, so it opens.","Sentence 1 says \"These seedlings,\" so the seedlings must be mentioned first, in sentence 4.","\"Their roots\" in sentence 3 refers to planted seedlings, so 3 comes after 1."],"wrong":{"2-1-4-3":"\"These seedlings\" in 1 has nothing to refer to if 4 has not come yet.","4-2-1-3":"Sentence 4 says \"The council,\" which needs sentence 2 to introduce it first.","2-4-3-1":"Roots (3) cannot hold soil before the seedlings are planted (1)."},"tip":"Words like \"these,\" \"their,\" and \"this\" point back to something already mentioned."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. Faced with these fines, most vendors now wrap goods in paper instead of plastic.\n2. The town ordinance passed in March bans single-use plastic bags in all public markets.\n3. However, some shoppers complain that paper bags tear easily when wet.\n4. To answer these complaints, the market office now sells sturdy woven bags at cost.\n5. Under this ordinance, vendors caught giving out plastic bags are fined five hundred pesos.","o":["2-1-5-3-4","5-2-1-3-4","2-5-1-3-4","2-5-3-1-4"],"a":2,"e":"2 states the ban, 5 gives the penalty under \"this ordinance,\" 1 refers back to \"these fines,\" 3 contrasts with a complaint about paper, and 4 answers \"these complaints.\"","x":{"steps":["Sentence 2 introduces the ordinance, so it opens.","\"Under this ordinance\" (5) explains the penalty right after it.","\"As a result\" (1) shows the effect of the ban and fine.","\"However\" (3) objects to paper bags, and 4 answers \"these complaints.\""],"wrong":{"2-1-5-3-4":"Placing 1 before 5 breaks the link: the result comes before its full cause, and 3's \"However\" would then contrast with the fine, not with paper wrapping.","5-2-1-3-4":"\"This ordinance\" in 5 needs the ordinance to be named first in 2.","2-5-3-1-4":"Sentence 3 complains about paper bags, so 1 (switching to paper) must come before it."},"tip":"Follow cause, result, problem, solution: \"As a result,\" \"However,\" and \"these complaints\" mark each step."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. Within an hour, the social welfare officer had called for volunteers to repack rice and canned goods.\n2. That evening, every family in the evacuation center had received a bag.\n3. Early that morning, news reached the municipal office that the river had flooded two sitios.\n4. In the afternoon, the bags were loaded onto trucks and brought to the evacuation center.\n5. By noon, the volunteers had packed more than three hundred relief bags.","o":["3-5-1-4-2","1-3-5-4-2","3-1-4-5-2","3-1-5-4-2"],"a":3,"e":"Time markers give the sequence: early morning (3), within an hour (1), by noon (5), afternoon (4), evening (2).","x":{"steps":["Underline each time marker.","Put them in order: early morning, within an hour, noon, afternoon, evening.","Match each time to its sentence: 3-1-5-4-2."],"wrong":{"3-5-1-4-2":"Volunteers cannot pack bags by noon (5) before they are called (1).","1-3-5-4-2":"\"Within an hour\" (1) needs an earlier event, the news in 3.","3-1-4-5-2":"Bags must be packed (5) before they are loaded onto trucks (4)."},"tip":"In a narrative, order the sentences by their time markers."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. For instance, a resident of a far-flung sitio can now request a barangay clearance without leaving home.\n2. Moreover, online payment has shortened the long lines that used to form at the treasurer's office.\n3. The town of Malaya del Norte has moved several of its frontline services online.\n4. In short, going digital has saved residents both time and fare money.\n5. The first benefit is convenience for residents who live far from the town center.","o":["3-5-1-2-4","3-1-5-2-4","5-3-1-2-4","3-5-2-1-4"],"a":0,"e":"3 states the topic, 5 names the first benefit, 1 gives an example of it, 2 adds a second benefit with \"Moreover,\" and 4 sums up with \"In short.\"","x":{"steps":["Sentence 3 introduces the topic, so it opens.","\"The first benefit\" (5) must come before \"Moreover\" (2), which adds another.","\"For instance\" (1) gives an example of convenience for far residents, so it follows 5.","\"In short\" (4) closes the paragraph."],"wrong":{"3-1-5-2-4":"\"For instance\" (1) needs a general point before it; that point is in 5.","5-3-1-2-4":"Sentence 5 says \"The first benefit,\" which needs the topic in 3 to be stated first.","3-5-2-1-4":"The example in 1 belongs to the first benefit (5), not after \"Moreover\" (2)."},"tip":"Topic, point, example, added point, summary: \"For instance\" and \"Moreover\" each need something before them."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Arrange the sentences to form a logical paragraph.\n1. Staff have been given one working day, Friday, to move the records and computers.\n2. Transactions will resume at the new location the following Monday.\n3. The Municipal Civil Registry will transfer to the new government center next week.\n4. Since the move will take that whole day, the office will be closed to the public.","o":["3-1-4-2","3-4-1-2","1-3-4-2","3-1-2-4"],"a":0,"e":"3 announces the transfer, 1 names the moving day, 4 refers to \"that whole day,\" and 2 gives the reopening \"the following Monday.\"","x":{"steps":["Sentence 3 announces the main news, so it opens.","Sentence 4 says \"that whole day,\" which refers to Friday in sentence 1, so 1 comes right before 4.","\"The following Monday\" (2) comes after Friday is mentioned and closes the notice."],"wrong":{"3-4-1-2":"\"That whole day\" in 4 has no day to refer to if 1 has not appeared yet.","1-3-4-2":"Sentence 1 mentions \"the records,\" but the reader must first know about the transfer in 3.","3-1-2-4":"If 2 comes before 4, \"that whole day\" would wrongly point to Monday."},"tip":"Find phrases like \"that day\" or \"the following Monday\" and place them right after what they point to."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?\n1. Composting turns kitchen and garden waste into natural fertilizer.\n2. Households that compost send less garbage to the town dump site.\n3. The dump site in our town was built by a private contractor many years ago.\n4. Plants grown in compost-rich soil also need less chemical fertilizer.","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"a":2,"e":"The paragraph is about the benefits of composting; sentence 3 is about the history of the dump site.","x":{"steps":["Find the main topic: composting and its benefits.","Check if each sentence supports that topic.","Sentence 3 talks about who built the dump site, which is off-topic."],"wrong":{"Sentence 1":"Sentence 1 introduces composting, the topic of the paragraph.","Sentence 2":"Sentence 2 gives a benefit of composting (less garbage).","Sentence 4":"Sentence 4 gives another benefit of composting (less chemical fertilizer)."},"tip":"A sentence can share a word with the topic (dump site) and still be off-topic."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?\n1. The provincial library now lends tablets to senior high school students for up to two weeks.\n2. Borrowers must present a school ID and a letter signed by a parent.\n3. Many students say they still prefer reading printed novels to reading e-books.\n4. A late fee of twenty pesos a day is charged for tablets returned after the due date.\n5. Damaged units must be reported to the librarian within twenty-four hours.","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"a":1,"e":"The paragraph explains the rules for borrowing tablets; sentence 3 is about students' reading preferences.","x":{"steps":["Sentence 1 sets the topic: lending tablets.","Sentences 2, 4 and 5 each give a borrowing rule.","Sentence 3 gives an opinion about reading, not a rule, so it does not belong."],"wrong":{"Sentence 2":"Sentence 2 gives a requirement for borrowing.","Sentence 4":"Sentence 4 gives the rule on late returns.","Sentence 5":"Sentence 5 gives the rule on damaged units."},"tip":"Name the paragraph's job in a few words, then test each sentence against it."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Which is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?\nBarangay Dalisay Norte used to flood every rainy season. Last year, residents began clearing the canals of garbage every Saturday. The barangay also banned the dumping of waste near the creek. This year, the streets stayed dry even during the heaviest rains.","o":["Many other barangays also experience flooding during typhoons.","The creek is home to many kinds of fish and birds.","Regular cleanups and strict rules can keep a community dry.","Next year, the barangay plans to build a new basketball court."],"a":2,"e":"The paragraph shows how cleanups and a dumping ban stopped the flooding; the conclusion should sum up that lesson.","x":{"steps":["Identify the main point: actions taken stopped the flooding.","A good conclusion restates or sums up that main point.","Pick the sentence that connects the actions to the result."],"wrong":{"Many other barangays also experience flooding during typhoons.":"This shifts to other barangays instead of concluding this story.","The creek is home to many kinds of fish and birds.":"Wildlife in the creek is a new topic.","Next year, the barangay plans to build a new basketball court.":"A basketball court has nothing to do with flooding."},"tip":"A concluding sentence sums up the paragraph; it does not open a new topic."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Paragraph organization","q":"Which is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?\nMany offices now allow employees to work from home two days a week. Supporters say this cuts travel time and lowers transport costs. Critics, however, worry that teamwork suffers when staff rarely meet in person. Some agencies have tried a middle path: fixed office days for meetings and home days for focused tasks.","o":["Working from home is clearly better than working in the office.","Employees should never be allowed to work from home.","Transport fares have gone up several times in the past decade.","A blended schedule may give workers the benefits of both arrangements."],"a":3,"e":"The paragraph presents both sides and ends with a middle path, so the best conclusion sums up that balanced idea.","x":{"steps":["Notice that the paragraph gives both sides: supporters and critics.","It ends with a \"middle path\" combining both.","The conclusion should match that balanced view."],"wrong":{"Working from home is clearly better than working in the office.":"This takes one side, but the paragraph is balanced.","Employees should never be allowed to work from home.":"This takes the opposite extreme and ignores the middle path.","Transport fares have gone up several times in the past decade.":"Transport fares are only a side detail, not the main point."},"tip":"When a paragraph weighs both sides, the best conclusion is balanced too."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nNOTICE TO RESIDENTS\nBarangay Mabuhay Silangan will hold a free anti-rabies vaccination for dogs and cats on Saturday, from 8:00 a.m. to 12:00 noon, at the covered court. Owners must bring their pets on a leash or in a cage. Pets below three months old will not be vaccinated. Owners are asked to bring their pet's previous vaccination card, if any.\n\nWhich pet will NOT be vaccinated during the activity?","o":["a two-month-old puppy on a leash","a one-year-old cat in a cage","a dog without a previous vaccination card","a four-month-old kitten in a cage"],"a":0,"e":"The notice says pets below three months old will not be vaccinated; a two-month-old puppy is too young.","x":{"steps":["Find the rule about which pets are excluded.","Pets below three months old are not vaccinated.","Only the two-month-old puppy falls below that age."],"wrong":{"a one-year-old cat in a cage":"A one-year-old cat in a cage meets all the requirements.","a dog without a previous vaccination card":"The card is needed only \"if any,\" so a dog without one may still be vaccinated.","a four-month-old kitten in a cage":"Four months is above the three-month minimum."},"tip":"Check every condition in the notice against each option."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nNOTICE TO RESIDENTS\nBarangay Mabuhay Silangan will hold a free anti-rabies vaccination for dogs and cats on Saturday, from 8:00 a.m. to 12:00 noon, at the covered court. Owners must bring their pets on a leash or in a cage. Pets below three months old will not be vaccinated. Owners are asked to bring their pet's previous vaccination card, if any.\n\nBased on the notice, which statement is most likely true?","o":["Owners will pay a small fee for each pet.","The activity will continue until the afternoon.","A pet with no vaccination card may still be vaccinated.","Only dogs are covered by the vaccination drive."],"a":2,"e":"The words \"if any\" show that the card is requested but not required.","x":{"steps":["Test each statement against the notice.","The notice says to bring the card \"if any,\" meaning only if the owner has one.","So a pet without a card can still be vaccinated."],"wrong":{"Owners will pay a small fee for each pet.":"The notice says the vaccination is free.","The activity will continue until the afternoon.":"It ends at 12:00 noon, not in the afternoon.","Only dogs are covered by the vaccination drive.":"The notice covers both dogs and cats."},"tip":"Small words like \"if any,\" \"only,\" and \"except\" often decide the answer."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nThe Provincial Scholarship Office announces that renewal of scholarship grants for the second semester is now open. Scholars must submit their grades for the first semester and a certificate of enrollment. Those with an average below 85 percent will be placed on probation rather than removed from the program, provided they have no failing grade. Incomplete submissions will not be processed.\n\nA scholar has an average of 83 percent and no failing grade. According to the announcement, what will happen to the scholar?","o":["The scholar will be removed from the program.","The scholar will be placed on probation.","The scholar's grant will be increased.","The scholar must repeat the semester."],"a":1,"e":"An average below 85 percent with no failing grade leads to probation, not removal.","x":{"steps":["Compare 83 percent with the 85 percent cutoff: it is below.","Check the condition: the scholar has no failing grade.","The announcement says such scholars are placed on probation."],"wrong":{"The scholar will be removed from the program.":"The announcement says \"rather than removed,\" so removal does not apply here.","The scholar's grant will be increased.":"Nothing in the announcement mentions increasing grants.","The scholar must repeat the semester.":"Repeating a semester is never mentioned."},"tip":"Apply the stated rule exactly; do not add outcomes the passage does not mention."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nThe Provincial Scholarship Office announces that renewal of scholarship grants for the second semester is now open. Scholars must submit their grades for the first semester and a certificate of enrollment. Those with an average below 85 percent will be placed on probation rather than removed from the program, provided they have no failing grade. Incomplete submissions will not be processed.\n\nAs used in the announcement, the word \"provided\" most nearly means:","o":["supplied","even though","in addition to","on the condition that"],"a":3,"e":"\"Provided they have no failing grade\" sets a condition for probation instead of removal, so it means \"on the condition that.\"","x":{"steps":["Read the full phrase: \"provided they have no failing grade.\"","It sets a requirement that must be met.","Replace it with each choice; only \"on the condition that\" keeps the meaning."],"wrong":{"supplied":"\"Supplied\" is another meaning of provided, but nothing is being given here.","even though":"\"Even though\" shows contrast, but the phrase sets a condition.","in addition to":"\"In addition to\" adds an item; the phrase states a requirement."},"tip":"Substitute each choice into the sentence; the right one keeps the meaning."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"easy","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nFor years, students at Tanglaw National High School threw their used paper into the same bins as food waste. Last school year, the student council placed separate bins for paper in every classroom and sold the collected paper to a junk shop each month. The money was used to buy electric fans for the library. Teachers noticed that the campus also became cleaner, since students began to pay more attention to where their trash went.\n\nWhat is the passage mainly about?","o":["why the library needed electric fans","how a junk shop buys used paper","why students should not eat inside classrooms","how a school's paper-sorting project brought several benefits"],"a":3,"e":"The passage describes the paper bins project and its results: money for fans and a cleaner campus.","x":{"steps":["Ask what the whole passage keeps talking about.","Every sentence relates to the separate paper bins and their effects.","Choose the option that covers the project and all its results."],"wrong":{"why the library needed electric fans":"The fans are only one result, a detail, not the main idea.","how a junk shop buys used paper":"The junk shop is mentioned briefly; the passage is not about how it works.","why students should not eat inside classrooms":"Eating in classrooms is never discussed."},"tip":"The main idea covers the whole passage, not just one sentence of it."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nFor years, students at Tanglaw National High School threw their used paper into the same bins as food waste. Last school year, the student council placed separate bins for paper in every classroom and sold the collected paper to a junk shop each month. The money was used to buy electric fans for the library. Teachers noticed that the campus also became cleaner, since students began to pay more attention to where their trash went.\n\nWhich of the following can be inferred from the passage?","o":["The junk shop paid more than the fans cost.","The project made students more careful about waste disposal.","The teachers ran the paper collection themselves.","Food waste was also sold to the junk shop."],"a":1,"e":"Since students \"began to pay more attention to where their trash went,\" the project made them more careful.","x":{"steps":["Look at the last sentence about students and their trash.","\"Began to pay more attention\" means their habits improved.","This supports the idea that students became more careful."],"wrong":{"The junk shop paid more than the fans cost.":"The passage gives no amounts, so we cannot compare earnings and costs.","The teachers ran the paper collection themselves.":"The student council, not the teachers, ran the project.","Food waste was also sold to the junk shop.":"Only collected paper was sold."},"tip":"A valid inference is supported by the text; it does not need facts the passage never gives."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nMEMORANDUM\nTo: All Department Heads\nFrom: Office of the Municipal Administrator\nSubject: Use of Official Vehicles\n\nEffective immediately, official vehicles may be used only for trips covered by an approved trip ticket. Drivers must log the odometer reading before and after each trip. Vehicles may not be brought home overnight unless the trip ends after 9:00 p.m. and the department head approves in writing.\n\nA driver finishes an approved trip at 10:30 p.m. Under what condition may he bring the vehicle home?","o":["if he logs the odometer reading the next morning","if the trip was in another province","if his department head gives written approval","if no other vehicle is available the next day"],"a":2,"e":"Both conditions must be met: the trip ends after 9:00 p.m. (it did) and the department head approves in writing.","x":{"steps":["Find the rule on bringing vehicles home: two conditions joined by \"and.\"","The first condition (trip ends after 9:00 p.m.) is already met at 10:30 p.m.","The remaining condition is written approval from the department head."],"wrong":{"if he logs the odometer reading the next morning":"Odometer readings must be logged before and after each trip; doing it the next morning is not the rule for overnight use.","if the trip was in another province":"The memo says nothing about trips to another province.","if no other vehicle is available the next day":"Vehicle availability is not one of the stated conditions."},"tip":"When a rule uses \"unless A and B,\" both A and B must be true."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nMEMORANDUM\nTo: All Department Heads\nFrom: Office of the Municipal Administrator\nSubject: Use of Official Vehicles\n\nEffective immediately, official vehicles may be used only for trips covered by an approved trip ticket. Drivers must log the odometer reading before and after each trip. Vehicles may not be brought home overnight unless the trip ends after 9:00 p.m. and the department head approves in writing.\n\nWhat is the main purpose of the memorandum?","o":["to set rules on how official vehicles are used","to announce the purchase of new vehicles","to assign drivers to each department","to explain how trip tickets are printed"],"a":0,"e":"The subject line and every sentence give rules for using official vehicles.","x":{"steps":["Read the subject line: \"Use of Official Vehicles.\"","Each sentence states a rule (trip ticket, odometer log, overnight use).","So the purpose is to set rules on vehicle use."],"wrong":{"to announce the purchase of new vehicles":"No purchase of vehicles is mentioned.","to assign drivers to each department":"The memo does not assign any drivers.","to explain how trip tickets are printed":"Trip tickets are required, but how they are printed is not explained."},"tip":"For memos, the subject line usually states the purpose."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"difficult","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nWhen Barangay Pag-asa Proper first proposed a weekly farmers' market, many store owners objected, fearing that the stalls would steal their customers. Two years later, the same store owners are among the market's strongest supporters. The Sunday crowds that come for fresh vegetables, they discovered, also stop by their stores for rice, soap, and cellphone load.\n\nWhy did the store owners change their opinion of the farmers' market?","o":["The barangay paid them to support it.","The farmers agreed to stop selling vegetables.","The market was moved to a different day.","The market drew shoppers who also bought from their stores."],"a":3,"e":"They discovered that the Sunday crowds also bought rice, soap and load from their stores, so the market helped their business.","x":{"steps":["Find their first fear: the market would steal customers.","Find what they discovered: the crowds also shopped at their stores.","Their fear proved wrong, so they changed their minds."],"wrong":{"The barangay paid them to support it.":"No payment is mentioned in the passage.","The farmers agreed to stop selling vegetables.":"The crowds still come \"for fresh vegetables,\" so the farmers still sell them.","The market was moved to a different day.":"Nothing says the market day changed."},"tip":"Compare the \"before\" and \"after\" in a passage to find the reason for a change."}},
  {"c":"verbal","d":"average","t":"Reading comprehension","q":"Read the passage, then answer the question.\n\nThe Rural Health Unit of San Rafael del Monte reminds the public that its dental services are now by appointment only. The change follows complaints from patients who had to wait for as long as five hours. Patients may book a slot by calling the health unit or by visiting in person on weekdays. Walk-in patients will still be accepted for emergencies such as severe toothache or swelling.\n\nWhich statement best reflects the reason for the new appointment system?","o":["Patients had been waiting too long to be served.","The dentist will now work only on weekends.","Emergency cases will no longer be accepted.","The health unit wants to charge higher fees."],"a":0,"e":"The notice says the change \"follows complaints from patients who had to wait for as long as five hours.\"","x":{"steps":["Look for the sentence that explains why the change was made.","\"The change follows complaints\" about waiting up to five hours.","So the reason is long waiting times."],"wrong":{"The dentist will now work only on weekends.":"Bookings are made on weekdays; nothing says the dentist works only on weekends.","Emergency cases will no longer be accepted.":"The notice says emergencies will still be accepted.","The health unit wants to charge higher fees.":"Fees are not mentioned in the notice."},"tip":"Words like \"follows,\" \"because,\" and \"due to\" point to the reason."}}
]
